<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-outcomes-week-10-11">
  <title>Week 10 and Week 11</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Outcome E5 was introduced in Week 8 and Week 9, and it will be repeated this block.
      You will have another opportunity to demonstrate it alongside the new outcomes.
    </p>
    <p>
      These weeks focus on evaluating indeterminate limits with L'Hôpital's Rule,
      applying the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to
      definite integrals whose antiderivatives involve exponentials and logarithms,
      and building right triangle models to solve applied problems with trigonometry.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E5-repeat">
    <title>E5 Logarithmic Differentiation (Repeated)</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E5-repeat">
      <title>E5</title>
      <p>
        I can use logarithmic differentiation on <m>y=f(x)</m> by differentiating <m>\ln(|y|)</m>
        with the chain rule to solve for <m>y' = f'(x)</m> after taking advantage of the expanded logarithm.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <remark>
      <p>
        This outcome is unchanged from Week 8 and Week 9.
        See <xref ref="subsec-E5"/> for the checkpoint practice problem.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E6">
    <title>E6 L'Hôpital's Rule</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E6">
      <title>E6</title>
      <p>
        I can apply L'Hôpital's rule for indeterminate limits of the form <m>0/0</m> or
        <m>\infty/\infty</m>, including rewriting other indeterminate forms so that
        L'Hôpital's rule can be used.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="E6_LHopitals_Rule">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{e^{-3x} - 1 + 3x}{x^2}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-I3">
    <title>I3 Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Part 2 with Exponentials</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-I3">
      <title>I3</title>
      <p>
        I can apply the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to calculate
        definite integrals of continuous functions by identifying antiderivatives involving
        exponentials and then evaluating its change.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="I3_FTC2_Exponentials">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\displaystyle \int_{-1}^{2} x e^{-x^2} \, dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-I4">
    <title>I4 Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Part 2 with Logarithms</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-I4">
      <title>I4</title>
      <p>
        I can apply the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to calculate
        definite integrals of continuous functions by identifying antiderivatives involving
        logarithms and then evaluating its change.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="I4_FTC2_Logarithms">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\displaystyle \int_0^2 \frac{4x}{x^2+1} \, dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-T1">
    <title>T1 Right Triangle Trigonometry</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-T1">
      <title>T1</title>
      <p>
        I can create a right triangle model for an applied problem involving lengths and angles
        and use basic trigonometric ratios to compute the length of an unmeasured side.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="T1_Right_Triangle_Trigonometry">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You are standing on one side of a river along a section where the river is exactly
          20 feet wide and flows in a perfectly straight path.
          Your friend is on the opposite bank, but you have to turn 50 degrees to the right
          to be looking directly at them.
          Use a triangle model and trigonometry to determine the distance between you.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

</section>
